WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
Leave some broth out for a few days,

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.160
and it turns cloudy with microbes.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.880
For a long time, many people thought those

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.080
microbes simply sprang from the broth itself, and

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.080
that disease came from bad air.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.560
Around 1860, Louis Pasteur tested the first idea

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.800
with a flask that let air in but

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.160
kept microbes out.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.040
Then Robert Koch worked out how to prove

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.680
that one particular microbe causes one particular disease.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.880
So how did their experiments establish germ theory?

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.340
Two older ideas stood in the way.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.900
Spontaneous generation was the idea that living things

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.500
can arise from non-living matter, like microbes from

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:43.060
broth.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.420
Miasma was the idea that disease comes from

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.220
bad air, given off by rotting matter.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.620
Germ theory says something different: infectious diseases are

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.060
caused by microbes and other pathogens, and microbes

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.460
come only from other microbes.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.860
To prove it, scientists needed two things.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.460
Show that microbes don't arise from the broth

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.300
itself.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.580
And show that a specific microbe causes a

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.140
specific disease.

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.766
Pasteur filled flasks with nutrient broth, then heated

00:01:16.927 --> 00:01:19.166
each neck and bent it into a long

00:01:19.327 --> 00:01:21.726
S-shaped curve, like a swan's neck.

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:24.766
He boiled the broth to kill any microbes

00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:25.566
already in it.

00:01:26.207 --> 00:01:28.206
The end of the neck stayed open, so

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:29.726
air could flow in and out.

00:01:30.287 --> 00:01:33.246
But dust and microbes are heavier than air,

00:01:33.327 --> 00:01:35.326
so they settled in the low bend and

00:01:35.447 --> 00:01:37.006
couldn't climb up to the broth.

00:01:39.034 --> 00:01:41.833
The broth stayed clear, with no growth, for

00:01:41.994 --> 00:01:42.553
months.

00:01:43.114 --> 00:01:47.273
That mattered, because supporters of spontaneous generation argued

00:01:47.354 --> 00:01:50.313
that boiling or sealing a flask destroyed or

00:01:50.474 --> 00:01:52.393
shut out something in the air that life

00:01:52.474 --> 00:01:52.953
needed.

00:01:53.594 --> 00:01:56.633
Here, the air could still reach the broth.

00:01:57.114 --> 00:02:00.153
Boiled broth in flasks with short, straight necks,

00:02:00.314 --> 00:02:03.673
open to falling dust, turned cloudy within days.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.380
Then came the critical step.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.820
Pasteur broke the neck off some swan-neck flasks,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.860
so dust could fall straight in.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.500
Within days, the same broth turned cloudy.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.740
So the broth could support growth.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.300
It just needed microbes to reach it.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.380
Same broth, same air.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.260
The only difference was whether airborne particles could

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.580
get to the broth, so the microbes came

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.220
from the air, not from the broth itself.

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:36.513
In exam terms, the independent variable was whether

00:02:36.594 --> 00:02:38.833
airborne particles could reach the broth.

00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:42.593
The dependent variable was microbial growth, seen as

00:02:42.674 --> 00:02:43.393
cloudiness.

00:02:44.034 --> 00:02:46.433
The broth, the boiling and the air were

00:02:46.554 --> 00:02:47.233
kept the same.

00:02:47.954 --> 00:02:50.513
So Pasteur showed that microbes come from other

00:02:50.594 --> 00:02:54.273
microbes, which is why boiling, sterilising and covering

00:02:54.354 --> 00:02:55.153
food work.

00:02:55.794 --> 00:02:58.753
But his flasks didn't link any particular microbe

00:02:58.834 --> 00:02:59.713
to a disease.

00:03:00.194 --> 00:03:01.393
That was Koch's job.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.640
In 1882, Koch announced the cause of tuberculosis,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.880
which he said caused about one in seven

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.640
of all deaths.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.600
First, he looked at tissue from many tuberculosis

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:15.640
cases.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.680
In every one, after staining, he found the

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.360
same thin, rod-shaped bacterium, and he didn't find

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.200
it in healthy tissue.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.520
Second, he grew it on its own, in

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.640
a pure culture, a culture containing only one

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.240
kind of microbe.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.360
That took weeks, because this bacterium grows very

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:35.000
slowly.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.720
Third, he gave the pure culture to healthy

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.520
guinea pigs, and they developed tuberculosis.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.200
Fourth, he recovered the same bacterium from those

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.080
sick animals.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:48.680
Why all four?

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.040
Finding a microbe in sick hosts only shows

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.040
it's there alongside the disease.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.200
It might have moved in after the disease

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.080
began.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.880
Giving the pure microbe to a healthy host,

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.280
and seeing the disease appear, shows that the

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.880
microbe causes it.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.920
Recovering it again rules out a different microbe

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:07.880
sneaking in.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.940
These four steps are called Koch's postulates, and

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.900
their logic is still the standard, but they

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.580
don't fit every pathogen.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.620
Viruses can't be grown on their own, because

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.060
they need living cells.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.500
Some bacteria, like the one that causes leprosy,

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.100
still can't be grown on their own in

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:27.660
a lab.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.860
Some healthy people carry a pathogen without getting

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.860
sick, which breaks the first step.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.380
And for a disease that only infects humans,

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.820
deliberately infecting a healthy person is rarely ethical.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.140
Today, scientists often detect a microbe's DNA instead

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:45.020
of growing it.

00:04:47.034 --> 00:04:48.353
Here's how to hold it.

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:52.553
Koch's postulates: find it, grow it, give it,

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:53.673
find it again.

00:04:53.914 --> 00:04:57.193
And the two scientists answered two different questions.

00:04:57.434 --> 00:04:59.913
Pasteur showed where microbes come from.

00:05:00.314 --> 00:05:03.673
Koch showed which microbe causes which disease.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.140
Two exam questions now.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:08.180
First.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.500
Sheep on a farm developed a lung disease.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.660
A scientist found bacterium X in the lungs

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.380
of all twenty sick sheep, and in none

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.820
of twenty healthy sheep.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.780
She grew X in pure culture and gave

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.380
it to five healthy sheep.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.780
Four of them developed the disease.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:29.300
Assess whether this evidence shows that X causes

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:30.100
the disease.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.020
Pause here to answer.

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:45.673
The evidence strongly supports it.

00:05:45.954 --> 00:05:48.193
X was in every sick sheep and no

00:05:48.274 --> 00:05:50.913
healthy one, it was grown pure, and it

00:05:50.994 --> 00:05:53.473
caused the disease in four of five healthy

00:05:53.554 --> 00:05:53.953
sheep.

00:05:54.594 --> 00:05:57.393
But step four is missing: X must be

00:05:57.514 --> 00:05:59.953
recovered from those newly sick sheep and shown

00:05:59.994 --> 00:06:00.833
to be the same.

00:06:01.474 --> 00:06:04.033
Saying the evidence already proves X causes the

00:06:04.114 --> 00:06:06.353
disease is the classic mistake.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:08.926
Second.

00:06:09.407 --> 00:06:12.606
Chlamydia pecorum is a bacterium that causes eye

00:06:12.687 --> 00:06:14.886
and urinary disease in koalas.

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:17.726
It grows only inside living cells.

00:06:18.287 --> 00:06:20.446
Koalas in New South Wales are listed as

00:06:20.527 --> 00:06:21.966
an endangered species.

00:06:22.607 --> 00:06:25.886
Explain two reasons why Koch's postulates are difficult

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:27.406
to apply to this disease.

00:06:27.967 --> 00:06:29.326
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:44.533
First, the bacterium grows only inside living cells,

00:06:44.614 --> 00:06:46.053
so it can't be grown in a pure

00:06:46.134 --> 00:06:49.013
culture on its own, which step two needs.

00:06:49.334 --> 00:06:53.733
Second, step three means deliberately infecting healthy koalas,

00:06:53.814 --> 00:06:55.813
which is hard to justify for an endangered

00:06:56.134 --> 00:06:56.813
species.

00:06:57.174 --> 00:06:59.733
Saying the postulates fail because Chlamydia is a

00:06:59.814 --> 00:07:02.253
virus is the classic mistake.

00:07:02.294 --> 00:07:05.093
It's a bacterium that needs host cells.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.900
So for a germ theory question, match each

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:12.340
scientist to the question he answered.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.660
Pasteur's flasks show that microbes come from other

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.340
microbes, because only particle access changed.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.140
Koch's four steps link one microbe to one

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.060
disease.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:25.180
And when a step can't be done, say

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.900
which step and why.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.900
Next, how a pathogen gets from one host

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:30.860
to the next.

00:07:32.867 --> 00:07:34.306
Here are the key notes.

00:07:34.547 --> 00:07:36.466
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:36.547 --> 00:07:37.346
to copy them.
